      "role": "user",
      "content": "that's much better. the progressive character actually sounds like they have a real philosophy now. \n\nbut looking at them side-by-side, it still feels pretty lopsided. the traditionalist talks about \"wisdom\" and \"resilience\" which are hard concepts to argue against, whereas the progressive is basically just saying \"the current way is wrong/unfair.\" it feels like the traditionalist has the moral high ground in the writing.\n\ncan you try to balance the \"intellectual weight\"? for the traditionalist, maybe lean into why change can actually be harmful or chaotic in the long run, not just about \"tradition.\" and for the progressive, can you make their argument even more sophisticated? like, instead of just saying the system is \"flawed,\" can they explain the specific logical or economic reason why the old way *cannot* work in a modern world? i want both characters to leave the audience thinking, \"wow, both of these people actually have a point.\"",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
